course: dictionary
lesson: 13484
title: Identifiers: names that tell things apart
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An identifier is a name, number, label or sign that shows exactly who or what something is. The word comes from «identify», which means to say which one it is. Context helps you see the meaning: «Your pupil number is 4721, so the office knows exactly who you are.» A pupil number is an identifier, because it points to one child and no one else.
explain: In computing, an identifier is the name you give to something in a program. «score» and «lives» are identifiers for variables, and the computer uses them to keep each one separate. Check the meaning by swapping it in: if «score» were a sign for a player's car, the sentence would not make sense, so it must be a name.
explain: Identifiers often have rules. In many programs they cannot have spaces, and they cannot start with a number. A barcode, a passport number and a username are all identifiers in everyday life, because each one shows exactly which thing it belongs to.
fact: An identifier is a name, number, label or sign that shows exactly who or what something is.
fact: A «unique identifier» belongs to only one thing, like a passport number.
fact: In computing, an identifier is a name given to a variable or function, such as «score».
fact: Identifiers usually cannot have spaces or start with a number, so «2nd_score» would break the rules.
fact: Barcodes, usernames and pupil numbers are all identifiers in everyday life.
exercise: «Each library book has a barcode, so the system can tell it apart from every other book.» What is the barcode? | A type of paper | A kind of shelf | A late fine | *An identifier that shows exactly which book it is
exercise: In a computer game, a player types «score» to store points. What is score here? | A sign for the player's car | *An identifier, the name of a variable | A spelling mistake | A number of lives
exercise: Which name breaks the rules for a variable identifier? | lives | score | *2nd_score | player1
test: «Your pupil number is 4721, so the office knows exactly who you are.» What is your pupil number? | A lunch order | A date of birth | A name for your teacher | *An identifier
test: Which of these is an everyday identifier? | A raincoat | A bicycle bell | A jumper | *A username
exam: Which statement about identifiers is true? | They are always long words | *They usually cannot have spaces | They are only used in maths | They can start with a number
